{{block center|<math>\frac{dc_1}{c_1}=\frac{\delta c_1}{c_1};\quad \frac{dc_2}{c_2}=\frac{\delta c_1}{c_1}+\frac{\delta c_2}{c_2};\dots\quad \frac{dc_n}{c_n}={}^n_1\left[\frac{\delta c}{c}\right];</math>}}
{{block center|<math>dA_1=\delta A_1;\ dA_2=dB_1+\delta A_2;\ \dots\ dA_n=dB_{n-1}+\delta A_n.</math>}}

Performing the necessary substitutions and summations, we get
{{block center|<math>dB_n=\begin{cases} {}^n_1[\Delta A]\frac{\delta c_1}{c_1}+{}^n_2[\Delta A]\frac{\delta c_2}{c_2}+\dots+\Delta A_n\frac{\delta c_n}{c_n} \\ +(1+{}^n_1[\Delta A\ \cot A]\sin 1'')\delta A_1+(1+{}^n_2[\Delta A\ \cot A]\sin 1'')\delta A_2 \\ \qquad+\dots+(1+\Delta A_n\cot A_n\sin 1'')\delta A_n. \end{cases}</math>}}
{{block center|<math>d\lambda_{n+1}=\begin{cases} {}^n_1[\Delta\lambda]\frac{\delta c_1}{c_1}+{}^n_2[\Delta\lambda]\frac{\delta c_2}{c_2}+\dots+\Delta\lambda_n\frac{\delta c_n}{c_n} \\ -\{{}^n_1[\Delta\lambda\tan A]\delta A_1+{}^n_2[\Delta\lambda\tan A]\delta A_2+\dots \\ \qquad+\Delta\lambda_n\tan A_n\delta A_n\}\sin 1'' \end{cases}</math>}}
{{block center|<math>dL_{n+1}=\begin{cases} {}^n_1[\Delta L]\frac{\delta c_1}{c_1}+{}^n_2[\Delta L]\frac{\delta c_2}{c_2}+\dots+\Delta L_n\frac{\delta c_n}{c_n} \\ +\{{}^n_1[\Delta L\cot A]\delta A_1+{}^n_2[\Delta L\cot A]\delta A_2+\dots \\ \qquad+\Delta L_n\cot A_n\delta A_n\}\sin 1''. \end{cases}</math>}}

Thus we have the following expression for any geodetic error:—
{{block center|<math>\mu_1\frac{\delta c_1}{c_1}+\dots+\mu_n\frac{\delta c_n}{c_n}+\phi_1\delta A_1+\dots+\phi_n\delta A_n=E,</math> (8)}}
where <math>\mu</math> and <math>\phi</math> represent the respective summations which are the coefficients of <math>\delta c</math> and <math>\delta A</math> in each instance but the first, in which 1

readings.c1s5:
is added to the summation in forming the coefficient of <math>\delta A</math>.

The angular errors <math>x</math>, <math>y</math> and <math>z</math> must now be introduced, in place of <math>\delta c</math> and <math>\delta A</math>, into the general expression, which will then take different forms, according as the route adopted for the line of traverse was the zigzag or the direct. In the former, the number of stations on the traverse is ordinarily the same as the number of triangles, and, whether or no, a common numerical notation may be adopted for both the traverse stations and the collateral triangles; thus the angular errors of every triangle enter the general expression in the form
{{block center|<math>\pm \phi x + \cot Y . \mu' y - \cot Z . \mu' z</math>,}}
in which <math>\mu' = \mu \sin 1''</math>, and the upper sign of <math>\phi</math> is taken if the triangle lies to the left, the lower if to the right, of the line of traverse. When the direct traverse is adopted, there are only half as many traverse stations as triangles, and therefore only half the number of <math>\mu</math>'s and <math>\phi</math>'s to determine; but it becomes necessary to adopt different numberings for the stations and the triangles, and the form of the coefficients of the angular errors alternates in successive triangles. Thus, if the <math>p</math>th triangle has no side on the line of the traverse but only an angle at the <math>l</math>th station, the form is
{{block center|<math>+ \phi_l . x_p + \cot \text{Y}_p . \mu'_l . y_p - \cot Z_p . \mu'_l . z_p</math>.}}
If the <math>q</math>th triangle has a side between the <math>l</math>th and the <math>(l+1)</math>th stations of the traverse, the form is
{{block center|<math>\cot X_q(\mu'_l - \mu'_{l+1})x_q + (\phi_l + \mu'_{l+1} \cot Y_q)y_q - (\phi_{l+1} - \mu'_l \cot Z_q)z_q</math>.}}

As each circuit has a right-hand and a left-hand branch, the errors of the angles are finally arranged so as to present equations of the general form
{{block center|<math>[ax+by+cz]_r - [ax+by+cz]_l = E</math>.}}

